19] that if $s, t\\geq 2$ and $\\mu\\neq r$, then $\\mu=-2, t=2$ and $G=\\overline{(s+1)K_2}$. For $\\mu=-t$ we verify this conjecture to be true. We further study the case in which $\\mu\\neq-t$ and confirm the conjecture provided $t^2-4\\mu^2t-4\\mu^3=0$. For the remaining possibility we determine the structure of a putative counterexample and relate its existence to the existence of a particular 2-class block design. It occurs that the smallest counterexample would have 1265 vertices.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSeattle, January 8–11, 2025 (https://www.ams.org/journals/abs/2025-46-01/1203.pdf)\nciteturn0search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; The distinguishing index gives a measure of symmetry in a graph. ... The Mycielskian of a graph G, is an extension of G introduced by Mycielski in ... Part of the conjecture of Alikhani and Soltani on graph\ncolorings was proved in 2022 by Boutin et al, and in 2023 Kennedy et al. proved the rest of\nThe distinguishing index gives a measure of symmetry in a graph. Given a graph G with no\nK_<sub>2</sub> component, a distinguishing edge coloring is a coloring of the edges of G such that\nno non-trivial automorphism preserves the edge coloring. The distinguishing index,\ndenoted Dist’(G), is the smallest number of colors needed for a distinguishing edge\ncoloring. The Mycielskian of a graph G, is an extension of G introduced by Mycielski in\n1955. In 2020, Alikhani and Soltani conjectured a bound on the distinguishing index of the\nMycielskian of G based on Dist’(G). Part of the conjecture of Alikhani and Soltani on graph\ncolorings was proved in 2022 by Boutin et al, and in 2023 Kennedy et al. proved the rest of\nthe conjecture. We extended these results, focusing on the distinguishing index of star\ngraphs and proved a new stricter bound.(Received September 23, 2024)1203-05-46267Steve Butler, Vera Choi, Joel Jeffries, Nina McCambridge, Asia Morgenstern*,  \nSamuel Orellana Mateo. #29 *Prime juggling patterns with a fixed state.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDistinguishing Generalized Mycielskian Graphs (https://arxiv.org/abs/2006.03739)\nciteturn0academia16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.4 years ago; This paper examines the distinguishing number of the traditional and generalized Mycielskian graphs. ... This result proves and exceeds a conjecture of Alikhani and Soltani.\nTitle: Distinguishing Generalized Mycielskian Graphs\nAuthors: Debra Boutin, Sally Cockburn, Lauren Keough, Sarah Loeb, K. E. Perry, Puck Rombach\nDate: Fri Jun  5 23:33:09 2020\n\nA graph $G$ is $d$-distinguishable if there is a coloring of the vertices with $d$ colors so that only the trivial automorphism preserves the color classes. The smallest such $d$ is the distinguishing number, $\\operatorname{Dist}(G)$. The Mycielskian $\\mu(G)$ of a graph $G$ is constructed by adding a shadow vertex $u_i$ for each vertex $v_i$ of $G$ and one additional vertex $w$ and adding edges so that $N(u_i)~=~N_G(v_i)~\\cup~\\{w\\}$. The generalized Mycielskian $\\mu_t(G)$ is a Mycielskian graph with $t$ layers of shadow vertices, each with edges to layers above and below. This paper examines the distinguishing number of the traditional and generalized Mycielskian graphs. Notably, if $G~\\neq ~K_1,~K_2$ and the number of isolated vertices in $\\mu_t(G)$ is at most $\\operatorname{Dist}(G)$, then $\\operatorname{Dist}(\\mu_t(G)) \\le \\operatorname{Dist}(G)$. This result proves and exceeds a conjecture of Alikhani and Soltani.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nZhang and Zhang's unimodality conjecture for Clar covering polynomials | MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/357859/zhang-and-zhang-s-unimodality-conjecture-for-clar-covering-p)\nciteturn0search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; # Zhang and Zhang's unimodality conjecture for Clar covering polynomials ... Let $R(H)$R(H) 