 vertex, $\\operatorname{Dist^{\\prime}}(μ(G)) \\le \\operatorname{Dist^{\\prime}}(G)$, exceeding their conjecture. We also prove analogous results about generalized Mycielskian graphs. Together with the work in 2022 of Boutin, Cockburn, Keough, Loeb, Perry, and Rombach this completes the conjecture of Alikhani and Soltani.\n\ngraph coloring--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) Distinguishing Generalized Mycielskian Graphs (https://www.academia.edu/144494075/Distinguishing_Generalized_Mycielskian_Graphs)\nciteturn0search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.4 years ago; Crawled: 5 months ago; In Theorem 1 (Section 4), we prove a statement that is slightly stronger than the above conjecture. ... We note that K2 is an unsurprising exception since µ(K2 ) = C5 is, in a sense, an exception among cycles, since it is the only cycle with distinguishing number 3 that is realizable as a Mycielskian graph. Furthermore, we proved that for generalized Mycielskian graphs with t > 1, the only exception is when µ(t) (G) has so many isolated vertices that their number exceeds Dist(G).\n A graph having no twins is said to be twin-free. For example, vertices v1 , v2 , and v3 in Figure 1 are mutually twin vertices; so are u1 , u2 , and u3 . If two vertices of a graph G are twins, then there is an automorphism of G that simply exchanges them and fixes the remaining vertices. Thus, a distinguishing coloring must give distinct colors to each vertex in a set of mutual twins. Letting µ(G) denote the (traditional) Mycielskian of a graph G, Alikhani and Soltani [4] proved in 2018 that if G has at least two vertices and is twin-free, then Dist(µ(G)) ≤ Dist(G) + 1. They then conjectured the following. Conjecture 1. [4] Let G be a connected graph of order n ≥ 3. Then Dist(µ(G)) ≤ Dist(G) except for a finite number of graphs. In Theorem 1 (Section 4), we prove a statement that is slightly stronger than the above conjecture. In particular, we show the conjecture is true for all graphs on at least 3 vertices, not only connected graphs. We also extend our results to generalized Mycielskian graphs. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nau:Wang_J in:math - SciRate Search (https://scirate.com/search?page=23&q=au%3AWang_J+in%3Amath)\nciteturn0search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; @misc{1912.07594, author = {Yuhong Yang and Jianfeng Wang and Qiongxiang Huang and Zoran Stanic}, title = {{O}n joins of a clique and a co-clique as star complements in regular graphs}, year = {2019}, eprint = {1912.07594}, note = {arXiv:1912.07594v3} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDetermining Number and Cost of Generalized Mycielskian Graphs (https://arxiv.org/abs/2007.15284)\nciteturn0academia19 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.3 years ago; Title: Determining Number and Cost of Generalized Mycielskian Graphs ... This paper examines the determining number and, when relevant, the cost of 2-distinguishing for Mycielskians and generalized Mycielskians of simple graphs with no isolated vertices.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) Symmetry Parameters for Mycielskian Graphs (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/349943900_Symmetry_Parameters_for_Mycielskian_Graphs)\nciteturn0search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.7 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; 2 Mycielskian Construction ... The theorem below, from [10], addresses the distinguishing number of My- ... Conjecture 4.5.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDetermining number and cost of generalized Mycielskian graphs - MaRDI portal (https://portal.mardi4nfdi.de/wiki/Determining_number_and_cost_of_generalized_Mycielskian_graphs)\nciteturn0search6 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.5 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; determining number Mycielskian graph graph distinguishing cost 